mastery.question_text:
Decide whether this mapping diagram is a function, and justify your answer using the definition of a function.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) ellipse (0.8 and 2);
\draw (3.4,0) ellipse (0.8 and 2);
\node at (0,2.35) {$x$};
\node at (3.4,2.35) {$y$};
\node (L0) at (0,1.3) {$0$};
\node (L1) at (0,0) {$1$};
\node (L2) at (0,-1.3) {$2$};
\node (R0) at (3.4,0.9) {$4$};
\node (R1) at (3.4,-0.9) {$6$};
\draw[->] (L1)--(R0);
\draw[->] (L1)--(R1);
\draw[->] (L0)--(R0);
\draw[->] (L2)--(R1);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

mastery.solution_text:
A function pairs each input with exactly one output.
The input $1$ has two arrows leaving it, to $4$ and to $6$, so it is paired with two outputs.
That breaks the definition, so it is not a function.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) ellipse (0.8 and 2);
\draw (3.4,0) ellipse (0.8 and 2);
\node at (0,2.35) {$x$};
\node at (3.4,2.35) {$y$};
\node (L0) at (0,1.3) {$0$};
\node (L1) at (0,0) {$1$};
\node (L2) at (0,-1.3) {$2$};
\node (R0) at (3.4,0.9) {$4$};
\node (R1) at (3.4,-0.9) {$6$};
\draw[->,red,line width=1.3pt] (L1)--(R0);
\draw[->,red,line width=1.3pt] (L1)--(R1);
\draw[->] (L0)--(R0);
\draw[->] (L2)--(R1);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
